@node AI Menu Using It
@section Using It

Right-click.  Everything cmacs adds hangs off a single top-level
@samp{AI} entry, so the menu you already had grows by exactly one line
however many actions are registered -- the context menu is shared
property, and org alone contributes a dozen items to it.

@example
AI > Ask AI  > Ask about this... / Summarize... / Rephrase... / ...
     Chat    > New chat with this / Send to an open chat... / ...
     Brigade > Spawn an agent on this... / Pin as agent context / ...
     Tools   > your own deftool forms
@end example

Four groups, in the same order everywhere:

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .82
@headitem Group @tab For
@item @samp{Ask AI} @tab answer something about this, in a result window
@item @samp{Chat} @tab carry this into a conversation that persists
@item @samp{Brigade} @tab hand this to an agent that works while you are
  not looking
@item @samp{Tools} @tab your own @code{cmacs-brigade-deftool} forms that
  opted in
@end multitable

When the whole menu comes to @code{cmacs-ai-menu-inline-limit} items or
fewer (5 by default), the group submenus are skipped and the actions sit
directly under @samp{AI}.  A submenu holding two entries is a click you
did not need.  Rename the top-level entry with
@code{cmacs-ai-menu-label}.

@subsection From the Keyboard

Nothing is mouse-only.  @code{cmacs-ai-menu-command-map} holds the
keyboard equivalents; cmacs does not claim a global prefix for it, so
bind it where you like:

@lisp
(global-set-key (kbd "C-c a") cmacs-ai-menu-command-map)
@end lisp

@multitable @columnfractions .10 .35 .55
@headitem Key @tab Command @tab Does
@item @kbd{a} @tab @code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick} @tab choose by completion
@item @kbd{m} @tab @code{cmacs-ai-menu} @tab pop the menu at point
@item @kbd{s} @tab @code{cmacs-ai-summarize} @tab summarize
@item @kbd{r} @tab @code{cmacs-ai-rephrase} @tab rephrase
@item @kbd{p} @tab @code{cmacs-ai-reply} @tab draft a reply
@item @kbd{e} @tab @code{cmacs-ai-explain} @tab explain
@item @kbd{?} @tab @code{cmacs-ai-ask} @tab ask a question about it
@end multitable

@code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick} is the one to reach for on a tty or under
Evil: it is completion all the way down and never needs a popup.

@subsection Every Action Is a Command

A keybinding needs a command, and an action is a plist in a registry --
so every shipped action also has a real named command, bindable and
@kbd{M-x}-able.  The Ask group's were always commands; these are the ones
that used to exist only as menu entries:

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Command @tab Action
@item @code{cmacs-ai-chat-with-this} @tab New chat with this
@item @code{cmacs-ai-send-to-open-chat} @tab Send to an open chat
@item @code{cmacs-ai-send-to-libreclaw} @tab Send to a libreclaw room
@item @code{cmacs-ai-spawn-agent} @tab Spawn an agent on this
@item @code{cmacs-ai-send-to-task} @tab Send to a running task
@item @code{cmacs-ai-pin-context} @tab Pin as agent context
@item @code{cmacs-ai-make-brigade-task} @tab Make this a brigade task
@end multitable

For anything registered later -- your own actions, and the deftools that
opted in with @code{:menu} -- there is no fixed key to bind, so use the
per-group pickers, which read the registry every time:

@multitable @columnfractions .45 .55
@headitem Command @tab Offers
@item @code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick-ask} @tab the Ask AI group
@item @code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick-chat} @tab the Chat group
@item @code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick-brigade} @tab the Brigade group
@item @code{cmacs-ai-menu-pick-tools} @tab the Tools group (your deftools)
@item @code{cmacs-ai-run-action} @tab any action, by name
@end multitable

@code{cmacs-ai-run-action} checks the action's @code{:applies} before
running it, so invoking @samp{Rewrite in place} on a read-only buffer
says so rather than failing somewhere less explicable.

@node AI Menu Text Operations
@section Summarize, Rephrase, Reply

@cindex summarize
@cindex rephrase
@cindex reply

The three operations that earn their place on highlighted text.

Each asks one question in the minibuffer -- what to focus on, what tone,
what you actually want to say -- and each treats @key{RET} on an empty
answer as ``use your judgement'':

@multitable @columnfractions .16 .40 .44
@headitem Operation @tab Prompt @tab @key{RET} means
@item summarize @tab @samp{Summarize -- focus on}
  @tab summarize it for someone new to it
@item rephrase @tab @samp{Rephrase -- how?}
  @tab clearer, tighter, more direct
@item reply @tab @samp{Reply -- what do you want to say?}
  @tab draft the reply you judge most appropriate
@item explain @tab @samp{Explain -- focus on}
  @tab what it does and why, minus the obvious
@item ask @tab @samp{Ask about this}
  @tab the most useful thing to know about it
@end multitable

History is per-operation, so last time's refinement is one @kbd{M-p}
away.

None of them touches your buffer.  @code{rephrase} deliberately
@emph{shows} you the rewrite rather than applying it; the in-place
version is @code{cmacs-ai-rewrite-region}, which has always been a
different command with different consequences, and appears on the menu
as @samp{Rewrite in place...}.

@node AI Menu Result Window
@section The Result Window

Output streams into a dedicated Org buffer, opened beside what you were
reading or beneath it depending on the frame:

@multitable @columnfractions .18 .82
@headitem Key @tab Does
@item @kbd{q} @tab cancel the request and close the window
@item @kbd{C-c C-k} @tab the same
@item @kbd{w} @tab copy the answer (without the header) to the kill ring
@item @kbd{g} @tab run the same action again
@end multitable

Both closing keys @emph{cancel}, not just hide.  A result window you
have dismissed is one you have stopped reading, and a model still
generating into it is spending money on nobody.

Placement is chosen from the frame, not hardcoded: at
@code{cmacs-ai-output-split-threshold} columns (150 by default) or wider
the window opens side by side, below it underneath.  Both are ordinary
@code{display-buffer} actions, so @code{display-buffer-alist} overrides
them in the usual way.

@node AI Menu Surfaces
@section Where It Works

Each surface below has a resolver, so the menu acts on the right thing
rather than on the buffer as a whole.

@multitable @columnfractions .34 .18 .48
@headitem Surface @tab Target kind @tab What the model sees
@item Any highlighted text @tab @code{region} @tab the selection
@item Diagnostics (flymake / flycheck) @tab @code{diagnostic}
  @tab the message plus surrounding lines
@item Compilation and grep buffers @tab @code{diagnostic}
  @tab the error and the notes around it
@item Backtraces and the debugger @tab @code{backtrace}
  @tab the whole backtrace
@item Terminals: vterm, eshell, term, shell, bacon, the REPLs
  @tab @code{terminal} @tab the last command and its output
@item dired and dirvish @tab @code{file} / @code{files}
  @tab the file at point, or every marked file
@item Diffs: @code{diff-mode}, magit, vc @tab @code{hunk}
  @tab the enclosing hunk, and only it
@item Org @tab @code{org-node} @tab the heading and its subtree
@item Org source blocks @tab @code{region} @tab the code, with its language
@item Mail folder listing (mu4e headers) @tab @code{mail-folder}
  @tab the message @emph{bodies}, read from the maildir
@item Mail: mu4e view, gnus, notmuch, rmail, GenMail @tab @code{mail}
  @tab the message
@item Chat: libreclaw rooms, cmacs-ai chats @tab @code{chat}
  @tab the conversation above point
@item eww and gsurf-lite @tab @code{url} @tab the rendered page text
@item gsurf (full WebKit) @tab @code{gsurf-page}
  @tab title and URL; the body via gsurf's own async fetch
@item Images and imgedit @tab @code{image}
  @tab the file, for the multimodal path
@item vidstudio timelines @tab @code{clip} @tab the project
@item The brigade dashboard @tab @code{task} @tab the task on this row
@item roamgraph @tab @code{roam-node}
  @tab the selected note, read from its file
@item gnuseye @tab @code{entity} @tab the selected entity
@item Calculator sheets @tab @code{formula} @tab the cell and the sheet
@item C sources @tab @code{c-symbol}
  @tab the symbol, its lines, its function
@item gowl client buffers @tab @code{gowl-client} @tab the mirrored window
@end multitable

Anywhere with no more specific answer falls back to the symbol at point,
then to the buffer, so the menu is never empty where it could be useful.

@subsection vterm and dirvish

@cindex vterm
@cindex dirvish

Both are external packages and neither is required.  The resolvers
declare them and decline cleanly when they are absent, so the same build
works with or without them.

@table @asis
@item vterm
Treated as a first-class terminal.  Terminals differ in how they track
prompts, so the resolver tries the mode's own prompt navigation first
(@code{vterm-previous-prompt}, @code{comint-previous-prompt},
@code{eshell-previous-prompt}) to find the @emph{previous} prompt -- the
one that issued the command whose output you are looking at, not the
empty current one.  vterm only tracks prompts with shell integration
configured; without it the resolver falls back to the last
@code{cmacs-ai-target-terminal-lines} lines (120 by default), which is
always right enough to be useful.

@item dirvish
Needs nothing special: its buffers stay @code{dired-mode} derived, so
marked files, the file at point and the directory header all behave
exactly as they do in dired.  The dirvish major-mode names are listed as
well, for the versions that put their own mode on side panes.
@end table

@subsection Mail Folders Read the Messages

A folder listing and a message are different targets, and conflating
them is how you get a ``summary'' of a mailbox assembled from nothing
but the From and Subject columns -- which is all a
@code{mu4e-headers-mode} buffer actually renders.

So acting on a folder listing reads the messages themselves, off the
maildir, one file per row.  Each contributes its From, Date, Subject, a
note if it carries @code{List-Unsubscribe} (bulk mail is worth knowing
about when triaging), and the first
@code{cmacs-ai-target-mail-body-chars} of body text.  Quoted-printable
and base64 parts are decoded, @code{text/plain} is preferred over the
@code{text/html} alternative, and extraction stops at the MIME boundary
so the two do not run together.

It is not a MIME parser and does not try to be.  It is enough for a
model to tell a bill from a newsletter, which is what a folder summary
needs.

The read is @emph{lazy}: the target carries a thunk, not text.
Resolvers run while the menu is being built, so reading bodies there
would make every right-click in mu4e pay for content nobody asked for.
Nothing touches the disk until an action actually runs.

Two bounds keep the prompt sane --
@code{cmacs-ai-target-mail-max-messages} (25) messages read in full, the
rest listed by subject, and @code{cmacs-ai-target-mail-body-chars}
(2000) per message, on top of the overall
@code{cmacs-ai-target-max-chars} clip.

@subsection In-Scene Menus

The libregnum surfaces -- roamgraph, gnuseye, the scene editor -- do not
use @code{context-menu-functions} at all: their right-click is a
ray-pick in the C input layer and they build their own menu for the node
that was hit.  They splice in one @samp{Ask AI...} entry that opens the
full AI menu for whatever their own resolver reports:

@lisp
(append items (and (fboundp 'cmacs-ai-menu-scene-items)
                   (cmacs-ai-menu-scene-items)))
@end lisp

The helper returns @code{nil} when nothing applies, so the call can be
unconditional.

@node AI Menu Display Backends
@section Display Backends

The menu draws correctly under every way cmacs can be displayed, and
callers never branch on which.

@multitable @columnfractions .22 .40 .38
@headitem Backend @tab Menu drawn by @tab Notes
@item pgtk / x / ns @tab @code{x-popup-menu}
  @tab a real GTK menu under pgtk
@item @command{emacs --lrg} @tab @code{lrg-popup-menu} (in-engine)
  @tab drawn by libregnum itself
@item tty @tab @code{x-popup-menu} (text menus) @tab Emacs's own tty menus
@item tty, no menus @tab @code{tmm-prompt} / @code{completing-read}
  @tab last resort
@end multitable

@strong{This is not cosmetic.}  Under @option{--lrg} a stock
@code{context-menu-mode} does @emph{nothing at all} -- not just for
cmacs, for every package.  Emacs routes @kbd{mouse-3} through
@code{x-popup-menu}, and the lrg backend's RIF @code{menu_show_hook}
(@code{lrg_menu_show} in @file{cmacs/lrgterm/cmacs-lrgterm.c})
deliberately returns ``nothing selected''; it has to exist, because
@file{menu.c} calls it unconditionally for window-system frames and a
NULL hook crashes, but it cannot show a menu.

So @kbd{mouse-3} is bound to @code{cmacs-menu-open-context-menu}, which
builds the same @code{context-menu-map} -- every
@code{context-menu-functions} entry still contributes, in the usual
order -- and pops it through the routing above.  Bind that command
yourself if you want right-click working under @option{--lrg} for menus
that have nothing to do with the AI.

Two API entry points, for alist menus and keymap menus:

@lisp
(cmacs-menu-popup POSITION MENU)        ; -> the chosen VALUE
(cmacs-menu-popup-keymap KEYMAP &optional POSITION) ; -> the chosen BINDING
@end lisp

@code{cmacs-menu-popup-keymap} returns a binding rather than an event
path, because the backends disagree about what they return and every
caller wants the same thing: something to @code{funcall}.

@code{cmacs-libregnum-popup-menu} remains as a thin wrapper over
@code{cmacs-menu-popup}; it is the name the libregnum-side callers
already use.  The flattening code moved to @file{cmacs-menu.el} so that
subsystems built @emph{without} @option{--with-cmacs-libregnum} can use
it rather than grow a second copy that drifts.

@node AI Menu Extending
@section Extending It

@subsection A New Surface: Write a Resolver

@lisp
(cmacs-ai-register-target-resolver
 :name 'my-log-viewer
 :modes '(my-log-mode)
 :order 25
 :resolve
 (lambda (_click)
   (cmacs-ai-target-create
    :kind 'diagnostic
    :label "log entry"
    :text (buffer-substring-no-properties
           (line-beginning-position) (line-end-position))
    :buffer (current-buffer))))
@end lisp

Resolvers run in @code{:order} (ascending) and the first non-@code{nil}
answer wins, so priority is explicit rather than an accident of load
order.  Returning @code{nil} means ``not mine'' and the next resolver is
tried, so a resolver may claim a mode cheaply and still decline for
positions it does not understand.  @code{:resolve} runs with point
already at the click, inside a @code{save-excursion}.

Reserved slots in the ordering:

@multitable @columnfractions .12 .22 .66
@headitem Order @tab Resolver @tab Why
@item 10 @tab region @tab highlighted text always wins
@item 15 @tab diagnostic @tab a squiggle beats the identifier under it
@item 20--30 @tab (surfaces) @tab where mode-specific resolvers belong
@item 85 @tab c-symbol @tab adds the enclosing function
@item 90 @tab symbol @tab the symbol at point
@item 95 @tab buffer @tab the catch-all; never @code{nil}
@end multitable

A resolver must be cheap and must not prompt: it runs while a menu is
being built, and under @option{--gowl} that is the compositor's main
thread.  One that signals is skipped with a message rather than taking
the menu down.

@subsection A New Capability: Write an Action

@lisp
(cmacs-ai-register-action
 :name 'my-lint-explainer
 :group 'ask
 :order 25
 :label "Explain this lint warning"
 :applies (lambda (target) (eq (cmacs-ai-target-kind target) 'diagnostic))
 :run (lambda (target)
        (cmacs-ai-textops-run 'explain target "Focus on how to fix it.")))
@end lisp

Actions @emph{do} get to prompt: they run from the command loop.
Re-registering a name replaces it, so reloading your init is idempotent.

@subsection A Brigade Tool, on the Menu

The symmetry the brigade already promises: one
@code{cmacs-brigade-deftool} form publishes a capability to in-process
agents, to CLI agents over the MCP relay, and to external MCP clients.
With @code{:menu} it reaches you as well -- no second registration, no
menu-specific code in the tool:

@lisp
(cmacs-brigade-deftool file-facts
  "Report size, mtime and line count for a path."
  ((path string "File to inspect"))
  :menu '(file files) :menu-label "File facts"
  (my/file-facts path))
@end lisp

@code{:menu t} offers the tool on anything; a list of target kinds
restricts it.  The tool's first parameter receives the rendered target
and the rest are read from the minibuffer.  A tool marked
@code{:destructive} or @code{:confirm} is confirmed before it runs -- a
menu click must not become a quieter way to do something the tool itself
considers worth asking about.  Asynchronous tools are not offered, since
their handler takes a @code{DONE} callback rather than returning a
result.

@node AI Menu Cost
@section Cost, and Why Nothing Fires

Every brigade entry on this menu @strong{composes}; none of them
@strong{fires}.

@samp{Spawn an agent on this} fills in the compose transient -- agent,
model, budget, tools, working directory, and your target as the prompt
-- and shows it to you.  It does not start a run.  @samp{Make this a
brigade task} appends to a plan file without queueing it.

A right-click is a cheap, half-deliberate gesture.  Starting paid work
off one is how you end up with a bill you did not intend, and the
compose path already takes this line for voice input for exactly the
same reason.

Two more bounds worth knowing:

@itemize @bullet
@item
@code{cmacs-ai-target-max-chars} (40000) clips target text middle-out,
head and tail kept.  Targets are routinely whole files or a terminal's
scrollback.
@item
@code{cmacs-ai-actions-pinned-max} (8) bounds @samp{Pin as agent
context}.  Pinned items are served to every agent start through the
brigade's own @code{context-provider} registry;
@kbd{M-x cmacs-ai-unpin-all} clears them.
@end itemize

@node AI Menu Providers
@section Providers and Keys

@cindex API key
@cindex HTTP 401

The text operations default to the @strong{@code{claude-code}} provider
with the @strong{@code{sonnet}} model, deliberately not to
@code{cmacs-ai-default-provider} (@code{claude}, the HTTP Anthropic API).

The reason is the failure mode.  These run off a right-click, and the
HTTP providers need an API key in the environment
(@env{ANTHROPIC_API_KEY}, @env{OPENAI_API_KEY}, @dots{}); without one, a
menu click answers with a bare @samp{HTTP 401} in the result window and
no hint as to why.  The CLI providers reuse the auth the @command{claude}
binary already holds and cost nothing per call.
@code{cmacs-ai-completion-provider} defaults the same way for the same
reason.

@code{cmacs-ai-textops-provider} is a @emph{symbol}, not a string --
@code{cmacs-ai-client-new} checks for one:

@lisp
;; Local and free; good for these short calls.
(setq cmacs-ai-textops-provider 'ollama
      cmacs-ai-textops-model "qwen3.5:9b")

;; The HTTP API, if you have a key exported.
(setq cmacs-ai-textops-provider 'claude
      cmacs-ai-textops-model nil)      ; nil = the provider's own default
@end lisp

Setting them to @code{nil} defers to @code{cmacs-ai-default-provider} and
@code{cmacs-ai-default-model}, which is what the rest of cmacs-ai uses.

