@end multitable

The text operations read their system prompts through the variable
@emph{symbol} each time, so customising one takes effect on the next
invocation rather than at load time.

@subsection Doom, Evil, and mouse-3

Doom leaves @kbd{mouse-3} bound to @code{mouse-save-then-kill} and does
not enable @code{context-menu-mode}, so the right-click never reaches
@code{context-menu-map} and every entry any package adds is invisible.
The bootstrap detects this and rebinds, once, only when something that
is not menu-shaped owns the binding -- a @kbd{mouse-3} already bound to
a keymap or to @code{context-menu-map} is left alone.

@code{cmacs-menu-claim-mouse-3} is the shared implementation;
cmacs-piper's TTS menu uses the same call for the same reason, so the
two cannot drift into disagreeing about when to rebind.  Set
@code{cmacs-menu-override-mouse-3} to @code{nil} to keep your binding;
the menu stays reachable from the keyboard.

@node AI Menu Migration
@section Migrating From the Old Region Menu

@file{cmacs-ai-context-menu.el} used to hold a small @samp{AI ...} entry
that appeared only over an active region -- and, because nothing ever
called @code{cmacs-ai-context-menu-install}, never appeared at all:
loading the file added the entry to no hook.

That file is now a shim.  @code{cmacs-ai-context-menu-install} still
works and calls @code{cmacs-ai-menu-bootstrap};
@code{cmacs-ai-context-menu} is an obsolete alias for
@code{cmacs-ai-menu-populate}.  Nothing to change in an existing init,
and the four commands it offered (explain, rewrite, document, generate a
test) are all still on the menu -- now in every buffer rather than only
over a region.

@subsection Testing

@file{test/cmacs/cmacs-ai-menu-tests.el} covers all four layers: menu
flattening and backend routing (with @code{x-popup-menu} and
